(2223899, 303173, 43753, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by persimon:</b></div><hr/><i> Don''t mean to seem obtuse, but I don''t understand the title. I like the reflections and the contrast introduced by the bright snow. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Ahhh! Thanks for explaining your title. Not that I let my understanding of the title influence my score. I don''t give titles a lot of thought. This helps me understand more about you though:-)</td>', 'Originally posted by persimon: Don''t mean to seem obtuse, but I don''t understand the title. I like the reflections and the contrast introduced by the bright snow.   Ahhh! Thanks for explaining your title. Not that I let my understanding of the title influence my score. I don''t give titles a lot of thought. This helps me understand more about you though:-)', '2006-03-13 07:13:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223903, 303452, 42198, '<td valign="top">JohnBeebe summed it up. This is technically a great image, and is very emotive. It''s one of these out-of-the-box images (for DPC anyway) that voters will either love or hate.<br/> <br/>Personally, I loved it. I can appreciate the trouble you went to to set this up, and I think it was very underrated in the voting. I don''t see anything technically wrong with it, and can''t give any advise how to ''improve'' it. It''s just the luck of the draw.<br/> <br/>The only thing I will say is that when I first saw the image, I assumed it was blown out / over exposed. I didn''t realise you''d used white paint to get that effect.</td>', 'JohnBeebe summed it up. This is technically a great image, and is very emotive. It''s one of these out-of-the-box images (for DPC anyway) that voters will either love or hate. Personally, I loved it. I can appreciate the trouble you went to to set this up, and I think it was very underrated in the voting. I don''t see anything technically wrong with it, and can''t give any advise how to ''improve'' it. It''s just the luck of the draw. The only thing I will say is that when I first saw the image, I assumed it was blown out / over exposed. I didn''t realise you''d used white paint to get that effect.', '2006-03-13 07:17:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223908, 303526, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> Very unique composition. Somehow its inverse of regular blur-to-focus shots for such subjects.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Well exposed, colors are crisp, contrast is just fine .. which means most of the in-camera stuff is just fine. Though I feel post-processing need a bit of work (not technically though). I''ll save it for next section :)<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> Well, good thing about square crop is that anything would fit into it :) ..so no comments.<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Well, the image seems to be devided into two parts when I give it quick glance and the focus is at the extreme ends. This does not let me focus on one point and shift my focus from one corner to other. Also the tilt gives a kind of uncomfortable feeling (well, that might be because I''m used to horitontal horizon :)). Overall a nice attempt in trying to make something ordinary look "different" but somehow lacks the punch.<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Aesthetic: Very unique composition. Somehow its inverse of regular blur-to-focus shots for such subjects. Technical: Well exposed, colors are crisp, contrast is just fine .. which means most of the in-camera stuff is just fine. Though I feel post-processing need a bit of work (not technically though). I''ll save it for next section :) Challenge: Well, good thing about square crop is that anything would fit into it :) ..so no comments. Conclusion: Well, the image seems to be devided into two parts when I give it quick glance and the focus is at the extreme ends. This does not let me focus on one point and shift my focus from one corner to other. Also the tilt gives a kind of uncomfortable feeling (well, that might be because I''m used to horitontal horizon :)). Overall a nice attempt in trying to make something ordinary look "different" but somehow lacks the punch. I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej', '2006-03-13 07:18:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2223913, 303427, 37430, '<td valign="top"><i>Greetings from the Critique Club!</i> <br/> <br/><b>Aesthetic:</b> I dont know what so say about this one - this seems to be somewhere between planned and candid shot. It looks very pleasing as colors compliment each other and the focus is as such that it draws my eye to the subject.<br/> <br/><b>Technical:</b> Well exposed, good DOF, crip colors. Thats it.<br/> <br/><b>Challenge:</b> The best thing about square crop is that anything would fit into it ...so no comments on this one :)<br/> <br/><b>Conclusion:</b> Ummm... I feel this is above average composition and should have scored a lit higher. As already said, the DOF is such that the attention is drawn to subject, which is good :) Another good thing is that the subject (Do I sound too "robot" when I use word "subject" for humans???) has natural frame would it. I dont if smile would have added a little more to this one :)<br/> <br/>I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM.<br/> <br/>Wish you a great day!<br/><b><i>-Tej</i></b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  Aesthetic: I dont know what so say about this one - this seems to be somewhere between planned and candid shot. It looks very pleasing as colors compliment each other and the focus is as such that it draws my eye to the subject. Technical: Well exposed, good DOF, crip colors. Thats it. Challenge: The best thing about square crop is that anything would fit into it ...so no comments on this one :) Conclusion: Ummm... I feel this is above average composition and should have scored a lit higher. As already said, the DOF is such that the attention is drawn to subject, which is good :) Another good thing is that the subject (Do I sound too "robot" when I use word "subject" for humans???) has natural frame would it. I dont if smile would have added a little more to this one :) I''m by no means expert in area of photography. My comments are purely based on my limited understanding of technology and art behind digital photography. Apart from doing detailed evaluation of your submission, my intent is to learn different styles  of photography by critically evaluating selective submissions. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via email or PM. Wish you a great day!-Tej ', '2006-03-13 07:26:17.000000', NULL, 0),
