(2228328, 302624, 42100, '<td valign="top">I meant to comment on this one during the challenge, it is a very beautiful photograph.  The colors and tones are very nice, as is the offset composition.  <br/> <br/>Maybe it was easier than it looked to get in position, but I sure would like to be a fly on the wall, watching you trying to beat the timer.</td>', 'I meant to comment on this one during the challenge, it is a very beautiful photograph.  The colors and tones are very nice, as is the offset composition.   Maybe it was easier than it looked to get in position, but I sure would like to be a fly on the wall, watching you trying to beat the timer.', '2006-03-14 22:30:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(2228462, 303646, 35334, '<td valign="top">Sherryl,<br/> <br/>I thought this photo was pretty good for the square crop challenge. (I gave it a 5.) The powerful wooden forms on the left interact with the jumble of smaller wooden shapes on the lower right. There is another dynamic relationship between the green leaves in the left foreground and those at a distance in the upper right. The square shape reinforces these relationships--and thus makes this a good entry for the challenge.<br/> <br/>So why didn''t it do better in the challenge? I''ve looked at the original that you posted and I think that the basic problem is the lighting rather than over processing. You shot with fill flash but it was too strong to just be a fill for the other light. The wooden shapes on the lower right are very harshly lit with highlights that are almost burned out and rims of dark shadows behind them. You have lost the nice texture that is apparent in the trunks on the left.<br/> <br/>This is supposed to be an ancient forest. The natural light in a forest is filtered through the trees and is usually very soft. The light from the flash destroys the forest mood and makes the image look very unnatural.<br/> <br/>You also used fill flash on your Odd Couple image. Please also read my comment on it.<br/> <br/>--Dan</td>', 'Sherryl, I thought this photo was pretty good for the square crop challenge. (I gave it a 5.) The powerful wooden forms on the left interact with the jumble of smaller wooden shapes on the lower right. There is another dynamic relationship between the green leaves in the left foreground and those at a distance in the upper right. The square shape reinforces these relationships--and thus makes this a good entry for the challenge. So why didn''t it do better in the challenge? I''ve looked at the original that you posted and I think that the basic problem is the lighting rather than over processing. You shot with fill flash but it was too strong to just be a fill for the other light. The wooden shapes on the lower right are very harshly lit with highlights that are almost burned out and rims of dark shadows behind them. You have lost the nice texture that is apparent in the trunks on the left. This is supposed to be an ancient forest. The natural light in a forest is filtered through the trees and is usually very soft. The light from the flash destroys the forest mood and makes the image look very unnatural. You also used fill flash on your Odd Couple image. Please also read my comment on it. --Dan', '2006-03-14 23:21:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2229086, 303079, 31403, '<td valign="top">149 is sick and wrong for this outstanding photo - this is terrific work no matter what the other 190 or so voters say - I''m a fan</td>', '149 is sick and wrong for this outstanding photo - this is terrific work no matter what the other 190 or so voters say - I''m a fan', '2006-03-15 01:48:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(2229108, 303589, 31403, '<td valign="top">in reviewing my comments, I found a rather glib one I made for this photo - I think I owe you a decent comment.<br/> <br/>I think this is a beautiful photo, love the whole feeling of warmth that comes from it.<br/> <br/>How would I make it better?  I would try pushing the saturation of the blues, and I would tweak the cyan over toward the blue side (+3 or so).  I think I might crop it tighter.  Right now it feels like a photo of a tree with a sunset in the background a little - maybe cropping more to the sun and leaving some of the dark dark shapes out would focus the image more on the sunset?<br/> <br/>Of course this is just me talking off the top of my head.  I would print this and display it proudly in my home or office if I were you, it''s really nice work.</td>', 'in reviewing my comments, I found a rather glib one I made for this photo - I think I owe you a decent comment. I think this is a beautiful photo, love the whole feeling of warmth that comes from it. How would I make it better?  I would try pushing the saturation of the blues, and I would tweak the cyan over toward the blue side (+3 or so).  I think I might crop it tighter.  Right now it feels like a photo of a tree with a sunset in the background a little - maybe cropping more to the sun and leaving some of the dark dark shapes out would focus the image more on the sunset? Of course this is just me talking off the top of my head.  I would print this and display it proudly in my home or office if I were you, it''s really nice work.', '2006-03-15 02:00:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(2229162, 303446, 46045, '<td valign="top">ahh, your black and white conversion looks spectacular.</td>', 'ahh, your black and white conversion looks spectacular.', '2006-03-15 02:43:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2229715, 302462, 9821, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club ...<br/> <br/>Very nice, as the other commentors said, she''s extremely cute. I like the scene, it fits the challenge, and I don''t mind the border (although the border didn''t add anything to the photo either).<br/> <br/>Some other things I notice: The top of her head is cut off. Sometimes this is ok, but in this case is more looks like you just missed the shot you were looking for. Her arms are a little awkward, and the subject being dead center give the feel of "snapshot" rather than "candid moment". Perhaps taking a series of photos in a row would have helped you get a slightly better overall composition.<br/> <br/>Still, a nice job.<br/> <br/>milo</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club ... Very nice, as the other commentors said, she''s extremely cute. I like the scene, it fits the challenge, and I don''t mind the border (although the border didn''t add anything to the photo either). Some other things I notice: The top of her head is cut off. Sometimes this is ok, but in this case is more looks like you just missed the shot you were looking for. Her arms are a little awkward, and the subject being dead center give the feel of "snapshot" rather than "candid moment". Perhaps taking a series of photos in a row would have helped you get a slightly better overall composition. Still, a nice job. milo', '2006-03-15 08:22:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2229737, 303654, 50695, '<td valign="top">I was one of your 8s. I think the composition is interesting, with the steps(?) on the left and water curling around it. "freezing" the motion of the water was a good decision, as its shape is fascinating.</td>', 'I was one of your 8s. I think the composition is interesting, with the steps(?) on the left and water curling around it. "freezing" the motion of the water was a good decision, as its shape is fascinating.', '2006-03-15 08:36:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(2230118, 303582, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>What are the odds!  300+ images in the queue, and in a couple of days, you get me again as a critiquer!  I''m the lucky one, ''cause this is beautiful.  (I''m trying to reconcile the location with my recollections of Reykjavik, but, darn, I can''t.  It''s not that big pond in the middle of town, is it?)<br/> <br/>Okay, enough of that.  Down to business.  Beautiful.  Meets the Challenge perfectly, of course.  The leading lines of the fence posts take the viewer directly to the nicely delineated houses right back to the lovely expanse of water which your long exposure interpreted perfectly.  Keeping the viewer in the picture for another romp through.<br/> <br/>I have just one minor point.  Since this was an advanced editing Challenge, if it was mine, I''d probably given that last house on the right a little burn or the tiniest neutral paint. Just dull it down a tiny bit. As it is now, that house is the brightest point in the whole picture and is a bit of a sticking post.  The viewer does tend to go to the brightest area of the picture first.  <br/> <br/>Again, it was a pleasure to enjoy this image.  Keep ''em coming!<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  What are the odds!  300+ images in the queue, and in a couple of days, you get me again as a critiquer!  I''m the lucky one, ''cause this is beautiful.  (I''m trying to reconcile the location with my recollections of Reykjavik, but, darn, I can''t.  It''s not that big pond in the middle of town, is it?) Okay, enough of that.  Down to business.  Beautiful.  Meets the Challenge perfectly, of course.  The leading lines of the fence posts take the viewer directly to the nicely delineated houses right back to the lovely expanse of water which your long exposure interpreted perfectly.  Keeping the viewer in the picture for another romp through. I have just one minor point.  Since this was an advanced editing Challenge, if it was mine, I''d probably given that last house on the right a little burn or the tiniest neutral paint. Just dull it down a tiny bit. As it is now, that house is the brightest point in the whole picture and is a bit of a sticking post.  The viewer does tend to go to the brightest area of the picture first.   Again, it was a pleasure to enjoy this image.  Keep ''em coming! Alice', '2006-03-15 10:44:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2230211, 303434, 32644, '<td valign="top">Hey, congratulations. I just saw that you got 4th place in Square Crop. I don''t think I realized until now how many close ones you have. You are about due for a winner. Great work!<br/>Love, mom</td>', 'Hey, congratulations. I just saw that you got 4th place in Square Crop. I don''t think I realized until now how many close ones you have. You are about due for a winner. Great work!Love, mom', '2006-03-15 11:17:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(2230246, 303422, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Oh, nice!  My first thought on seeing this image was, "painterly."  Your treatment of the flowers and leaves does give a just painted feeling to this and I like that a lot.<br/> <br/>Certainly you met the Challenge with that ultra-square cropping and certainly the design is quite pleasing.  <br/> <br/>On composition, If I had to nit pic, I''d say that the right-in-the-middle stem is a touch awkward.  Had you slanted the stem &amp; flowers from (say) left to right, it might have been a more balanced composition.  And would have left less empty space at the top.  <br/> <br/>However, you did receive a respectable score, and that''s a plus!<br/> <br/>Finally, I''ll suggest that when you request a critique from the Critique Club, please do enter your camera settings.  It helps us give you the best comments we can, and it helps you understand your camera as well.<br/> <br/>I''ll look forward to more of your images in DPC.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Oh, nice!  My first thought on seeing this image was, "painterly."  Your treatment of the flowers and leaves does give a just painted feeling to this and I like that a lot. Certainly you met the Challenge with that ultra-square cropping and certainly the design is quite pleasing.   On composition, If I had to nit pic, I''d say that the right-in-the-middle stem is a touch awkward.  Had you slanted the stem & flowers from (say) left to right, it might have been a more balanced composition.  And would have left less empty space at the top.   However, you did receive a respectable score, and that''s a plus! Finally, I''ll suggest that when you request a critique from the Critique Club, please do enter your camera settings.  It helps us give you the best comments we can, and it helps you understand your camera as well. I''ll look forward to more of your images in DPC. Alice', '2006-03-15 11:33:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2230365, 302618, 55170, '<td valign="top">oh wow, where do i even begin?!??<br/>this is simply amazing.<br/>firstly, i LOVE the colours. &lt;3<br/>yep, who doesnt?<br/>the pastel and dreamy feeling as u''v mentioned....<br/>the bright and cheery feel.<br/>i can feel the fog; i can feel the weather then.<br/>great job.<br/>also, the composition is gd.<br/>not forgetting the square crop too!! ;)<br/>i feel that u''v utilised the square crop very well in this.<br/>think u captured it at the perfect moment as well:<br/>with the boy''s playful expression, that little jump, and the birds flying away from him.<br/>all is too wonderful for words.<br/>i fell in love at first sight with this photograph.<br/>great job. :D</td>', 'oh wow, where do i even begin?!??this is simply amazing.firstly, i LOVE the colours. <3yep, who doesnt?the pastel and dreamy feeling as u''v mentioned....the bright and cheery feel.i can feel the fog; i can feel the weather then.great job.also, the composition is gd.not forgetting the square crop too!! ;)i feel that u''v utilised the square crop very well in this.think u captured it at the perfect moment as well:with the boy''s playful expression, that little jump, and the birds flying away from him.all is too wonderful for words.i fell in love at first sight with this photograph.great job. :D', '2006-03-15 12:10:42.000000', NULL, 0),
